# DevGuard AI — backend environment.
# Copy to .env and fill in. Never commit .env itself.
#
#   cp .env.example .env

# ---- Required for live scans -------------------------------------------------
# Groq API key. Get one at https://console.groq.com/keys
# The backend now imports and boots WITHOUT this set — every endpoint that does
# not call an LLM works fine. It is required only when you run an actual scan.
GROQ_API_KEY=

# ---- Server ------------------------------------------------------------------
# Port the backend listens on. Must match NEXT_PUBLIC_API_BASE in the frontend
# and the published port in docker-compose.yml.
PORT=8000

# ---- Cache (optional) --------------------------------------------------------
# Redis is optional. When it is unreachable the backend runs CACHE-LESS — every
# scan re-runs the pipeline. There is no in-process fallback cache; this line
# used to claim there was one. Scans still work, they just cost a full pipeline
# run every time (`backend/core/cache.py` fails open by design: a cache outage
# degrades to slower, never to broken).
#
# Under docker compose this is redis://redis:6379/0.
REDIS_URL=redis://localhost:6379/0

# ---- OpenTelemetry / SigNoz (optional) ---------------------------------------
# Where to export traces, metrics and logs. Leave unset to run without a
# collector — telemetry is fail-safe and a scan never depends on it.
#   local collector (docker compose --profile obs):  http://localhost:4317
#   SigNoz Cloud:                                    https://ingest.<region>.signoz.cloud:443
OTEL_EXPORTER_OTLP_ENDPOINT=http://localhost:4317
OTEL_SERVICE_NAME=devguard-backend

# For SigNoz Cloud only — the ingestion key header.
# OTEL_EXPORTER_OTLP_HEADERS=signoz-access-token=<your-ingestion-key>

# Set to true to disable telemetry export entirely (useful for tests/CI).
# OTEL_SDK_DISABLED=true

# ---- SigNoz MCP (optional) ---------------------------------------------------
# Base URL of the SigNoz MCP server used by the self-observation layer.
#
# DELIBERATELY LEFT UNSET — do not uncomment unless you actually run an MCP
# server at the address you set. This line previously shipped as
# `SIGNOZ_MCP_URL=http://localhost:8080`, so `cp .env.example .env` (the very
# first step of the README quickstart) flipped `mcp_client.is_configured()` to
# True and pointed the self-observation layer at a port nothing serves. That
# undoes the whole point of the unset default: an unconfigured MCP path reports
# itself as unconfigured, instead of as a live integration that happens to be
# failing.
#
# This integration is NOT verified against a real SigNoz MCP server — see
# backend/core/mcp_client.py and docs/MCP_DECISION.md. Real MCP is JSON-RPC 2.0;
# this adapter assumes an HTTP envelope. When it is unreachable the cost path
# falls back to an in-process estimate, reported with data_source
# "local_shadow", never "live". Check the current state with:
#     curl localhost:8000/telemetry-status
#
# SIGNOZ_MCP_URL=

# ---- Access control (optional) -----------------------------------------------
# Shared secrets for the endpoints that spend money and for the approval gate:
# POST /scan, /scan/zip, /scan/repository, /scan/{id}/approve, /scan/{id}/reject.
#
# DELIBERATELY LEFT UNSET. Unset means no authentication, which is what makes a
# clean clone work with no configuration — every quickstart in the README runs
# without a key. That also means an internet-facing instance with this unset is
# open to anyone who can reach it.
#
# Comma-separated. Each key must be at least 16 characters; shorter ones are
# refused at load rather than silently accepted, because a 4-character key reads
# as protection without being any. Send it as either header:
#     Authorization: Bearer <key>
#     X-API-Key: <key>
# Check which mode is live (never prints the key itself):
#     curl localhost:8000/slo-status | jq .access_control
#
# DEVGUARD_API_KEYS=

# Comma-separated CORS allowlist. Unset allows every origin.
# DEVGUARD_ALLOWED_ORIGINS=https://your-frontend.example.com

# Per-client cap on the scan endpoints. 0 disables the limiter.
# DEVGUARD_SCAN_RATE_LIMIT=20
# DEVGUARD_SCAN_RATE_WINDOW_S=60

# Datasets DevGuard may write to. Unset uses the built-in five that match the
# DataHub Access Policy. Separated by WHITESPACE, not commas — a dataset URN
# contains commas of its own, so a comma-joined list is one malformed token,
# which is rejected rather than stored.
#
# Fails closed: any entry that is not exactly one dataset URN is dropped, and a
# wholly invalid value permits no dataset write at all. The server-side Access
# Policy remains the real control; this is the application-layer half.
#
# DEVGUARD_MUTATION_SCOPE_URNS="urn:li:dataset:(urn:li:dataPlatform:postgres,db.schema.tbl,PROD)
#                               urn:li:dataset:(urn:li:dataPlatform:postgres,db.schema.other,PROD)"

# ---- DataHub (required for a LIVE run; not needed for replay) -----------------
# Every variable below was verified against a running DataHub v1.7.0 during the
# session recorded in evidence/datahub-live/. Each line names the module that
# reads it, so a reader can check rather than trust.
#
# The Command Center replays committed proof packs with NONE of this set. These
# are needed only to drive a real catalog.

# Where GMS answers. `/config` is unauthenticated on every DataHub deployment,
# which is how backend/v2/datahub_preflight.py tells "no GMS here" apart from
# "GMS is here and rejected my token" — two different problems that a single
# error message would send you to debug in the wrong order.
#   quickstart default: http://localhost:8080
# Read by: datahub_preflight.resolve_gms_url, datahub_client.DataHubMCPClient
DATAHUB_GMS_URL=http://localhost:8080

# GraphQL, if it is not at ${DATAHUB_GMS_URL}/api/graphql. Accepted because a
# real deployment can put GraphQL behind a different host or path, and hard-coding
# the derivation would make that deployment unconfigurable.
# Read by: datahub_preflight.resolve_graphql_url
# DATAHUB_GRAPHQL_URL=

# The token, as a PATH TO A FILE — the preferred form, and the one every script
# in this repository is run with. Keep the file outside the repository, `chmod
# 600`. Tokens are read at runtime, never logged, and stripped from proof packs
# by backend/v2/proofpack.py.
#
# Generate one for the least-privilege service account (NOT for an admin):
#   python scripts/setup_service_account.py
#   python scripts/verify_least_privilege.py     # expects ALLOW 5/5, DENY 7/7
#
# Read by: datahub_preflight.resolve_token, datahub_client.load_token
# DATAHUB_TOKEN_FILE=/etc/devguard/datahub-agent.token

# The token inline. Both names are read, `DATAHUB_TOKEN` first, because it is
# the name an operator reaches for and setting it used to produce a silently
# UNAUTHENTICATED client whose failure surfaced much later as a permissions
# error against a server that was configured correctly.
#
# Prefer DATAHUB_TOKEN_FILE. A secret in an env var ends up in `ps`, in shell
# history, and in container inspect output.
# DATAHUB_TOKEN=
# DATAHUB_GMS_TOKEN=

# NOT a variable you need to set: datahub_client sets
# DATAHUB_TELEMETRY_ENABLED=false unconditionally on the MCP subprocess.
# Without it the server blocks ~90 s per call retrying telemetry POSTs that a
# restricted network 403s at CONNECT, and every call looks like a hang. Worth
# exporting for the `datahub` CLI too, which is not covered by that default:
#   export DATAHUB_TELEMETRY_ENABLED=false

# ---- DataHub UI capture (optional; only for scripts/capture_*) ---------------
# The frontend, for scripts/capture_datahub_screenshots.py. The quickstart's
# local credentials are a development default, not a secret — but they are read
# from the environment anyway, so an operator pointing this at a real catalog
# never has a committed password win over their own.
# DATAHUB_FRONTEND_URL=http://localhost:9002
# DATAHUB_USER=datahub
# DATAHUB_PASSWORD=

# Path to a Chromium binary, when Playwright's own download is unavailable or
# the installed browser build does not match the Python package's expectation.
# DEVGUARD_CHROMIUM_PATH=/opt/pw-browsers/chromium/chrome-linux/chrome
